# Both editions or neither

`legacy-windows/` is a second implementation of this core in TypeScript, shipped
to agencies on Windows 7 who cannot run the app at all. It answers the same 73
commands to the same screens, so a rule changed here and not there is not a
missing feature: it is two copies of the book behaving differently on the same
spreadsheet, and nothing raises an error when they do.

Change the counterpart in the same commit, and port the test that proves it.

Do not write one side and then read it to write the other. The two cores live in
different files, so they can be built at the same moment in the same checkout.
Write the rules down first, then use the `parallel-feature` skill to build both
halves from that one description at once.

| Changed | Also change |
| --- | --- |
| `commands.rs`, the `generate_handler!` list in `lib.rs` | `legacy-windows/src/core/commands.ts` |
| `models.rs` | `legacy-windows/src/core/types.ts` |
| `error.rs`, `query.rs`, `util.rs` | `core/errors.ts`, `core/query.ts`, `core/util.ts` |
| `repo/*.rs` | `core/repo/*.ts` of the same name |
| `importer.rs`, `exporter.rs`, `templating.rs`, `reminders.rs`, `mail.rs` | `core/` file of the same name |
| `tray.rs`, `scheduler.rs` | `src/shell.ts`, the sweep timer in `src/main.ts` |
| `tests.rs` | The `src/tests/*.test.ts` case naming that Rust test |

## What is already shared, and must not be copied

The schema and the interface. `legacy-windows` reads
`src-tauri/src/db/schema/*.sql` and builds the app's own `src/` through Vite
aliases, so a migration or a screen arrives there on the next build. Copying
either creates the drift this rule exists to prevent.

A migration is written once but has to work twice: the edition bundles an older
SQLite than the app, so anything resting on a version's behaviour rather than on
what SQLite promises can hold in one and not the other. `004_search_index.sql`
fixes an FTS trigger with a `WHEN` clause instead of reordering two triggers,
because the order they fire in is not promised and the two editions need not
agree on it.

## What deliberately differs

Encryption — the edition opens a plain file and `session.ts` answers
`encrypted: false`, and screens read that flag rather than assuming. Sending,
which is `nodemailer` against `lettre`. Paths, secrets and notifications, which
live in `src/env.ts` rather than in `core/`. Do not port `vault.rs`.

## Proving it

```bash
npm run check:all      # or `cd legacy-windows && npm test` for this edition alone
```

`parity.test.ts` reads the command list out of `lib.rs` and fails if the two
disagree about a name; the rest hold the ported rules to the Rust cases. CI runs
this only when an edition release is tagged, so `.githooks/pre-push` is where a
broken port is caught.

It is also the only drift any test can see. A rule implemented differently on
the two sides passes both suites, so run the `parity-auditor` subagent over the
diff before committing a change that touched a rule.
